In article <C21J9E.4zo@news.iastate.edu> john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) writes:
>}The university may own the machine, but the contents of its hard-drive
>}(or at least, some of the contents of the hard-drive) are probably owned
>}by the individual using the machine--not the university.
>
>And conversely, if the university has a policy of using university
>computers only for university duties, then, by defintion, the entire
>contents of the drive are owned by the university (and thus, searchable
>at their discretion).

Absoultely not.  If I teach a course at a university, and prepare lecture
notes for that course, I retain ownership of those notes.  A university
does not gain ownership of something simply because it is related to
"university duties."

Furthermore, I doubt very many universities have strict policies against
having any personal material on a university-owned computer.  For example,
most universities seem to accept non-university-related exchange of email,
or participation in, say, alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk.


