From caf-talk Caf Feb  8 11:32:48 1993
Newsgroups: misc.int-property,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: greeny@eff.org (J S Greenfield)
Subject: Article 3--Re: SPA vs. Universities
Message-ID: <1993Feb8.163241.13161@eff.org>
Date: Mon, 8 Feb 1993 16:32:41 GMT

In article <C24z0M.30r@ms.uky.edu> morgan@ms.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>
>As I said in another posting, I wouldn't be worried about this at all
>if SPA would nail individuals for violations.  If, however, the SPA
>insists on nailing the *university* for Professor Googolplex' illegal
>copy of SigmaPlot (tm) because the *university* owns the PC, don't we 
>deserve *some* means of protection and/or reduced liability?

I, for one, have very serious doubts about the legal basis for SPA targetting
the university.  For example, suppose that the university maintains a policy
against use of pirated software, notifies users of the policy and law in this
regard on a regular basis (perhaps even placing a sticker on all of its
computers to remind users), and takes appropriate action when violations
come to its attention.  Given that there are strong arguments that 1) regular
searches bring up invasion of privacy problems, and 2) regular searches are
very costly, I suspect that this would be enough to counter any negligence
arguments.  (I presume that SPA's position holding the university liable,
where the university did not knowingly tolerate or encourage the piracy, is
based on some theory that the university was negligent in preventing the
piracy.)

If this were the case, then all it would take would be for one university
(or other institution) to stand up to the SPA in order to establish a
precedent.  Therein lies the rub--since most institutions will probably
go for the greatest short-term benefit (balancing the cost of a legal defense
against extortion payments to SPA) rather than also considering the long-term
costs of privacy invasion and expensive regular monitoring of all machines.

Dare I suggest that, perhaps, the Copyright Act could be amended to explicitly
relieve institutions of liability for individual transgressions, provided
that there was no toleration or encouragement of such transgressions?
(Basically, if we can't get some institution to litigate to create a common law
precedent, then legislate it.)

Then, the burden would be placed upon the SPA to demonstrate that an
institution *had* knowingly encouraged or tolerated piracy.  Given that this
would be a much tougher proposition, the SPA would probably shrink back to
going after individual pirates and institutions where they could easily
show that the behavior was knowingly tolerated or encouraged.


