Academic institutions are not the government.  They are perfectly
capable of suspending priveledges until the hearing, and should.
The other difference between the two is suspension of rights vs.
continuation of priveledge.  When found guilty the gov't tosses
you in prison -- suspending your right to travel freely as part
of your punishment.  If the acad. institution locked you in the
room until the hearing you might have a point.  Restricting access
to a valuable resource -- that other students could make good use
of in the meantime -- until the matter has been determined is
not the same thing at all.

