Hmm.  You're probably about the *only* person at Syracuse to whom this was
"stated very clearly."  Usually, SU just has some fine print on the form
referring people to other policies.

But, in fact, I have the SU Academic Computing Services Computer Use Policy
in front of me (yes, I will type it in and post it to caf-talk when I get
a chance).  It cites the following as "examples of improper and irresponsible
use" (in brief form):

  --Unnecessary network traffic
  --Harassment--including sending obscene, threatening, or repeated,
      unnecessary messages
  --Maliciously destroying material or spreading viruses
  --Creating or using a trojan horse
  --Sending chain letters
  --Using obscene or inappropriate file names

I don't see anything about disclaimers, hate mail (note "hate mail" does
not necessarily equal "threatening" mail), slander--and certainly nothing about
"civil rights violations.

What's more, SU has several regulations explicitly protecting students' (and
others) right to free expression.  And the school's definition for "harassment"
(from the Code of Student Conduct) is consistent with the legal definition.
The Code prohibits:

  Harassment, whether physical or verbal, oral or written, which is 
  beyond the bounds of protected free speech, directed at a specific 
  individual(s), easily construed as 'fighting words,' and likely to 
  cause an immediate breach of the peace.


As such, it should be clear that if the behavior is legal, it is protected
by SU regulations.

As for disclaimers, I believe it is clearly a mistake to take their presence
on the net as a concession that institutions are, generally, liable for what
individuals post.  The intention is to try to avoid headaches, nothing more.
(And it applies to more than just legal issues.)


