Actually, you quite clearly *do* pay for your access.  This access isn't
given to just anybody.  Access to the computer equipment is one of the
benefits of your enrollment contract.  You pay tuition (or provide some
other consideration) in exchange for many rights--including the right to
attend classes, be eligible for a degree, use the library, have access to
computers, and much more.

Furthermore, you clearly do not understand the law.  I haven't seen the
post in question (could somebody please repost it, for those of us who have
not seen it), but I am hard-pressed to imagine how it could possibly have
constituted a "civil rights violation."

In any case, even if we give you the benefit of the doubt and assume that,
somehow, it could have been a "civil rights violation" the school could
still not be held liable unless it had a priori knowledge that the material
was such, and failed to do anything about it.


